\section{On paper}{


\describe{
\item{\code{addr_strict()}}{The RFC dotted-quad grammar: exactly four decimal
octets, no leading zeros, no hex, no octal, no short form. This is what
Python's \code{ipaddress}, Go and Rust accept. It is \strong{not} what
\code{inet_pton()} accepts, although the two are often conflated.}
\item{\code{addr_whatwg()}}{The WHATWG URL host parser -- what browsers do. Hex
and octal parts, one to four parts with the last filling the remainder,
and one trailing dot dropped, so \verb{1.2.3.} is \verb{1.2.0.3}. Values above
2^32 - 1 are rejected rather than wrapped.}
}
}

\section{In reality}{


\describe{
\item{\code{addr_pton()}}{Apple \code{inet_pton()}. Four decimal parts, leading zeros
allowed and ignored, so \verb{0177.0.0.1} is \strong{177.0.0.1} and not
\verb{127.0.0.1}.}
\item{\code{addr_aton()}}{Apple \code{inet_aton()}. Hex, octal and short forms, and
three quirks worth knowing: a whole-host number is truncated to 32 bits
rather than rejected, so \code{4294967296} is \verb{0.0.0.0}; parsing stops at the
first whitespace character and ignores the rest, so \verb{1.2.3.4 junk} is an
address; and a digitless \verb{0x} is tolerated in any part but the last.
\code{inet_aton()} is \code{AF_INET} by signature, so it rejects \strong{every} IPv6
literal.}
}

Both say \emph{Apple} rather than POSIX or BSD, and that is load-bearing. Measured
across Apple, glibc and musl on 2026-07-29, there is no reality-side reading
the three libcs agree on: glibc and musl \code{inet_pton()} \strong{reject} every
leading zero above, and their \code{inet_aton()} rejects every overflow rather
than wrapping it. raddr models Apple on all four reality-side readings, as a
dialect that varied with the host would not be a function -- and reports that
choice here rather than implying a standard it does not have.
}

\section{IPv6}{


The shape of the disagreement inverts. The two paper dialects agree about
IPv6 on every measured input, and all of the divergence is on the reality
side:

\describe{
\item{Leading zeros}{A hextet is four hex digits on paper. Apple
\code{inet_pton()} counts only the \emph{significant} four and lets the zeros run
as wide as they like, so \verb{0000000000001::} is \verb{1::} where \code{strict} and
\code{whatwg} reject. The dotted-quad tail splits the same way.}
\item{The zone ID}{The paper dialects have none: RFC 4291's grammar does
not admit one and the WHATWG parser rejects \verb{\%}. The reality dialects
accept a zone on any address and resolve nothing, so \verb{\%bogus0} parses.
The zone is stored beside the bits and read with \code{\link[=addr_zone]{addr_zone()}}; it never
enters the address and never affects equality.}
\item{\verb{fe80::/10}}{\code{addr_getaddrinfo()} lifts the second hextet of a
link-local address out into the zone and clears it, zone ID or not, so
\verb{fe80:abcd::1} is \verb{fe80::1} with zone \code{43981} -- while \code{addr_pton()}
leaves it alone. One string, one machine, two different hosts.
\code{addr_curl()} goes with \code{getaddrinfo}, because that is the entry point
curl reaches.}
}

Apple \code{inet_pton()} also does the reverse, writing a resolved interface index
\emph{into} the second hextet. raddr deliberately does not reproduce that: the
index comes from the host's interface table, so it is not a function of the
input, and raddr is pure and offline.

The lift is Apple's own, measured 2026-07-29. glibc and musl do not
perform it -- \verb{fe80:abcd::1} stays \verb{fe80:abcd::1} there -- and their
\code{inet_pton()} takes no zone ID at all, so the fold cannot arise either. That
makes \code{addr_getaddrinfo()} and \code{addr_curl()} Apple readings across the whole
of \verb{fe80::/10} rather than at its edges. Outside that block the platforms
agree.
}

\section{Compositions}{


The last two are precedence orderings over the reality primitives, not
parsers in their own right:

\describe{
\item{\code{addr_getaddrinfo()}}{\code{pton}, falling back to \code{aton}. Whitespace is
the one place the composition leaks: \code{getaddrinfo()} rejects an input
containing whitespace outright, where bare \code{aton} would accept it.}
\item{\code{addr_curl()}}{\code{aton}, falling back to \strong{\code{addr_getaddrinfo()}} --
the opposite precedence, which is the whole reason \verb{192.0.048.1} reaches
a host under curl that a browser refuses to dial.}
}

The asymmetry in that second fallback is not a slip. curl's URL layer
normalizes a numeric host itself, \code{aton}-style, and hands the resolver
whatever is left, so the fallback is the resolver entry point rather than the
bare parser under it. For IPv4 the distinction is invisible, because
\code{getaddrinfo()} reduces to \code{pton} once \code{aton} has rejected. For IPv6 it is
the whole composition: \code{aton} rejects every IPv6 literal, so \verb{fe80:abcd::1}
is \verb{fe80::1} with zone \code{43981} under \code{addr_curl()}, exactly as under
\code{addr_getaddrinfo()}.
}

\section{What these do not give you}{


These are shortcuts for a caller who has already chosen a dialect. They
return a bare address, so a rejected input comes back as \code{NA} with no reason
attached. The total, outcome-bearing form -- every reading at once, with the
reason codes -- is \code{\link[=addr_parse]{addr_parse()}}, and its result is what \code{\link[=addr_reading]{addr_reading()}}
reads a single dialect back out of.
}

\section{Provenance}{


The reality dialects and \code{addr_getaddrinfo()} were measured against Apple
libc on macOS Darwin 25.4.0 arm64 on 2026-07-26. \code{addr_curl()}'s precedence
was measured against real curl 8.20.0 on 2026-07-28 -- until then it was
derived from the other two rather than run, and the IPv6 half of it was
wrong. \code{data-raw/oracle-ipv4.py} and \code{data-raw/oracle-tools.R} regenerate the
measurements; \code{tests/testthat/test-ipv4.R} holds them as the divergence
table.

The same corpus was run under glibc 2.36 and musl 1.2.5 on 2026-07-29 by
\code{data-raw/oracle-libc-linux.sh}, which is what fixes these functions to Apple
rather than to a standard. Those fixtures are recorded, never modelled;
\code{tests/testthat/test-libc.R} asserts the divergence set so a libc upgrade
shows up as a changed file.
}

